course: dictionary
lesson: 7085
title: Pay in context
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Pay has more than one meaning, so context tells you which one is right. «Before a loaf of bread reaches you, many people work on it, and each of them is paid from the price you pay.» Here pay means to give money for something you buy, and paid means the workers receive money for their work.
explain: Pay can also be a noun. Pay is the money a person gets for work. Try swapping in your meaning: if «pay» means «give money», does the sentence still make sense? If it does, you have the right meaning.
explain: Some phrases have meanings that have nothing to do with money. «Pay attention» means listen or look carefully, and «pay a visit» means visit. Read the words around pay, and the other words will tell you which meaning to use.
fact: To pay means to give money for something you buy or for work done.
fact: Pay (as a noun) is the money a person gets for work.
fact: Pays N for each of K means N × K: if you pay 2 pence for each of 3 pencils, you pay 2 × 3 = 6 pence.
fact: Change is the money you give minus the cost: if you pay 10 pence for a 6 pence sweet, your change is 4 pence.
fact: To pay attention means to listen or look carefully. To pay a visit means to visit.
exercise: «Before a loaf of bread reaches you, many people work on it, and each of them is paid from the price you pay.» What does paid mean here? | Given away for free | *Received money for work done | Carried to the shop | Put in a bag
exercise: Mia says, «Pay attention to the bell.» What does pay attention mean? | Ring the bell loudly | *Listen carefully | Hand over money | Visit the bell
exercise: Sam buys 3 pencils at 2 pence each. How much does he pay in all? | 1 penny | 5 pence | 23 pence | *6 pence
test: «Fixed costs are bills that come every month, like rent; they are paid before money goes on fun.» What are fixed costs? | Prizes at a fair | Free games | *Bills that come every month, like rent | Things you find in a shop
test: Which sentence uses pay as a noun, meaning money earned for work? | We pay for lunch at the café | He is paying attention | *Her pay arrived on Friday | The shop will pay you a visit
exam: «At the ancient Olympic Games, cheats paid fines.» What does paid fines mean here? | Carved names into stone | *Gave money as a punishment | Looked after the statues | Won medals for running
